Reccs for set up: 
* Put the llms.txt at the root domain (appcues.com/llms.txt), vs a subdirectory. This is the standard location models and crawlers check first
* Add a <link rel="alternate" type="text/plain" href="/llms.txt"> tag to the homepage <head> so crawlers find it automatically
* While we are updating our entire docs.appcues.com, CS/engineer should add a one-line config change to create markdown (MD) versions of each page. Besides the llm.txt this is the part of the site where product detail lives and models will likely pull from- this gives them clean/structured content to cite rather than having to parse HTML
   * every page on docs.airops.com has 2 things: "For the complete documentation index, see llms.txt" and "This page is also available as Markdown."


How LLMs learn about our product 


#1 AI search tools (Perplexity, SearchGPT, Google AI overviews, ChatGPT with web search)
These actively crawl the web and can surface new content pretty quickly (like days or weeks after something is published, similar to how Google indexes new pages).


When our new llms.txt is live and our docs are in clean markdown (MD), these tools can read and cite them almost immediately. The new site being live on 7/14 is the real trigger here–once that content is indexed, AI search tools will start picking up the new ways we’re describing ourselves. 


#2 Base model training (Claude, GPT-4, Gemini, etc.)


This is the slower one! when someone asks Claude a question without web search enabled, he’s drawing on training data with a fixed cutoff (for Sonnet 4.6 that is August 2025). 


Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google periodically retrain their models on new data from the web, but that happens on a cycle of months to over a year vs real time. The new Appcues positioning won't show up in a base model's answers until the next model trained after our content has been crawled and included in a training dataset, which could be anywhere from 6 to 18+ months from now depending on the model provider.


