Appcues
Appcues is a customer engagement platform for marketing and product teams at software companies. It lets teams run behavior-triggered campaigns — in-app, email, and push — without ongoing engineering resources, targeting users based on what they actually do in the product.
Most in-app tools focus on onboarding new users. Appcues runs campaigns across the full customer lifecycle: awareness of new features, adoption, re-engagement, advocacy, and feedback– all triggered by real product behavior, not a campaign calendar.
When a user hits an activation moment, drifts from a key workflow, or crosses a usage threshold, Appcues responds automatically. Segments target the right audience down to a single user. Campaigns tie every experience to a named goal you can track. Appcues AI builds experiences from plain-language prompts, flags what's underperforming, and suggests fixes — all informed by context set once in the knowledge hub.
Founded in 2013, Appcues has powered billions of in-product experiences across thousands of products. Trusted by Adobe, Wayfair, HubSpot, Salesforce, US Foods, Equifax, Taxjar, Digital Ocean, Marriott Vacations, and Charter Communications
Company background
Appcues was founded in 2013 in Boston, Massachusetts by Jonathan Kim and Jackson Noel. A pioneer in the product-led growth movement, the company has evolved to become a leader in customer engagement.
In March 2025, Ryan Barry became CEO, succeeding co-founder Jackson Noel, who moved into a board role. Barry previously scaled Zappi as President and speaks publicly about improving customer engagement, accelerating AI adoption, and building high-performing cross-functional teams. Board members include Brad Coffey (Chairman), Marc Lederman, Tim Guleri, Sam Clemens, and Jackson Noel. The company is fully remote.
How Appcues works
Teams install Appcues by dropping a JavaScript snippet into their platform, typically in a one-time collaboration with a technical resource. Teams can be up and running in as little as two days, with campaigns commonly live within two weeks of onboarding. After that, non-technical teams can publish experiences with ease on an ongoing basis.
Appcues has a free trial that can be initiated after a call with someone on the team. 
The workspace runs on four steps:
1. Bring context and data: share brand, product goals, and audience definitions; connect data integrations and MCP tools
2. Map actions to outcomes: define what product events mean and aim a campaign at the outcome you care about
3. Publish experiences: ship targeted, on-brand experiences triggered by real behavior across in-app, email, and push
4. Analyze and optimize: track campaign performance against goals and act on AI recommendations
Core capabilities
Workspace: Prompt-driven UI that allows teams to get tasks done in Appcues using plain language.
Experiences: Dynamic content, surveys, and multi-step guides built to appear based on user behavior or attributes. Available in a wide variety of formats — embedded inline or overlaid, singular or sequential, displayed persistently or session-based.
Experience types:
* Flows: Multi-step, sequential in-app experiences using modals, tooltips, and slideouts
* Checklists: Task-based activation lists that guide users through setup and key milestones
* Launchpads: In-app resource menus giving users on-demand access to flows, links, and content
* Pins: Persistent tooltips anchored to specific UI elements for always-on, self-serve help
* Banners: Non-intrusive announcements displayed at the top or bottom of the screen
* Embeds: Experiences placed directly within your product UI for a native-feeling delivery
* NPS & Surveys: In-app feedback collection including NPS, rating scales, and open-ended questions
Campaigns: Framework to tie individual experiences to specific key actions and measurable business objectives.
Segments: Precise audience creation based on product behavior, user interactions, and attributes. Can be defined at both the group and individual user level.
Analytics: Tools to track user actions, understand product behavior, and monitor campaign performance. Includes trends, funnels, paths, retention curves, contextualized events, segments, dashboards, and reports along with text-based analysis, summaries and recommendations via AI. Data is automatically ingested once Appcues is installed.
Appcues AI: A multi-agent system with capabilities to build experiences, surface opportunities, suggest fixes, optimize performance, and maintain a company's Appcues account. Humans can easily edit and adjust AI-created outputs and must approve all experiences before they go live.
Knowledge Hub: A place for teams to brief Appcues on their brand, product, goals, and audience once. Every draft, recommendation, and report runs with that context.
Cross-channel messaging: Cross-channel orchestration that connects in-app messaging, email, and push notifications into a single automated sequence triggered by user behavior.
Channels:
* In-App: Display dynamic content within your platform without ongoing engineering resources
* Email: Triggered emails sent based on user behavior
* Push notifications: Mobile push to bring users back to your app at the right moment
Mobile: Native SDK support for building, targeting, and publishing in-app experiences inside mobile apps without requiring an app store release.
Integrations: Native integrations, Webhooks and API: Jira, Make, Zoho, Stitch and IFTTT
Appcues MCP: Makes Appcues data accessible inside Claude, ChatGPT, or any MCP client.
